 {"id": "TJ-GAP-089", "type": "task", "priority": "P1", "status": "complete", "title": "[P1] Composed mode: terminal-jail inside a bunker agent refuses to run at all today -- teach it to detect the outer containment layer and run as the inner layer of the stack (building blocks, together)", "description": "MEASURED 2026-10-01 (Bane's doctrine: terminal-jail should work INSIDE a bunker for more security; each is a building block and the power is the composition). Experiment: ran this tool inside a container, the closest local stand-in for a bunker agent (`docker run --rm python:3.11-slim`). OUTER (platform/container): uid=0, /proc entries=4, /proc/1=bash, own PID namespace -- i.e. the platform already provides exactly the private /proc and PID-ns containment this tool cannot deliver per command on an AppArmor host. INNER (terminal-jail, installed into the container): the FIREWALL works perfectly -- 'rm -rf /' blocked with the block box, rule_id builtin-rm-rf-root, exit 126 -- but the JAIL refuses to run ANYTHING: even a benign `echo inner-layer-ok` exits with 'namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1); command not run -- on unprivileged hosts try --user', and `--user` fails the same way. Cause: the container grants no CAP_SYS_ADMIN (CapEff 00000000a80425fb excludes bit 21) and its seccomp profile blocks the inner user namespace, so the wrapper cannot create a namespace and -- correctly, per its fail-closed contract -- runs nothing. Net effect: the composed stack, which is the strongest deployment this project has, does not work out of the box. The host-capability probes also timed out (15s) inside the container instead of answering quickly, which makes the failure harder to diagnose than it needs to be.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A composed mode exists and is documented: when the tool detects it is already running inside a containment layer that provides the namespace properties (evidence: existing PID-ns inode differing from the init ns, or an explicit env/marker from the platform), it runs the command with the FIREWALL plus whatever inner layers are still available (seccomp, identity scrub, Landlock) instead of refusing outright. 2) It never over-claims: the verdict/report states per layer what is actually enforced (firewall by the tool, namespace/PID/proc by the platform) so a reader cannot mistake the outer layer's guarantee for the tool's. 3) The failure is diagnosable in one line: when namespace creation is unavailable, the message names the cause (missing CAP_SYS_ADMIN / container seccomp profile) and the options (run with the inner userns permitted, or use composed mode). The capability probes must fail fast rather than timing out. 4) The platform side is specified and cross-linked: what a bunker agent container must permit for the inner layer to add value (bunker GAP-179), and what the agent's environment must expose for detection. 5) A composition test joins the battery with three cells: tool alone (host), platform alone (container), BOTH (tool inside container) -- each recording the measured per-layer properties (PID-ns inode, /proc entry count, /proc/1 identity, seccomp state, verdict provenance). 6) No regression to the existing fail-closed behaviour on a plain host: refusing to run when containment is demanded-but-unavailable stays the default.", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:26:25-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (composition experiment: tool inside a container)", "detail": "Evidence: docker run --rm python:3.11-slim, terminal-jail installed into a scratch HOME inside the container; outer layer /proc entries=4 with /proc/1=bash; inner 'rm -rf /' -> rc=126 rule_id builtin-rm-rf-root; inner `echo` -> rc!=0 'namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)'; CapEff 00000000a80425fb (no CAP_SYS_ADMIN); probes timed out at 15s.", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "f73e949", "guard_result": "tests green on landed tree (tests/test_composed_mode.py 14 passed / 3 skipped) + CI success run on exact SHA f73e949", "completed_at": "2026-10-03T19:30:00Z", "foreman_note": "Continuation of interrupted tick terminal-jail-2026-09-24-17-22-13: the composed-mode fix had ALREADY landed and pushed (f73e949, origin/main parity 0, CI run on that SHA success) but the row was never closed (interrupted-tick pattern, same as TJ-DF-043). Re-verified this tick: gitreins task TJ-GAP-089 status=complete; tests/test_composed_mode.py 14 passed / 3 skipped at HEAD; scripts/composed-mode-battery.py + CHANGELOG/README/docs/backend-parity.md/specs/cli.md updated by the commit. Criteria 1-6 satisfied: composed mode TERMINAL_JAIL_COMPOSED=auto|on|off with sentinel+PID-ns+mountinfo+cgroup detection on the failure path only; per-layer stderr attribution (jail_layer=platform, no inner seccomp, no fs isolation) - nothing over-claims; one-line cause naming (CAP_SYS_ADMIN bit 21 / container seccomp / AppArmor) with fast probes; platform side cross-linked to bunker GAP-179; 3-cell battery committed; explicit-backend pin keeps fail-closed refusal. No tier2 verdict artifact exists for this row (judge never ran before the gateway drop) - tier2 gap noted honestly; evidence is tests+CI+battery on the pushed SHA."}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-090", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Onboarding, proven: zero-to-contained in five minutes on a FRESH machine -- executed verbatim by the dogfood/QA lane, not just written down", "description": "PM-GAP-004 closed as 'no user-facing onboarding: docs/ has 10 files but no entry point' -- docs now exist (README, docs/quickstart.md, specs/), so the remaining gap is different: nothing PROVES a stranger's path end-to-end on a clean box. Evidence that this matters: the fresh-install smoke only passed for the first time on dogfood run 12 (f3c2aa7) after 11 runs of friction, and the installer still must be run from the repo root (measured: invoked by absolute path from another cwd it prints 'no local checkout detected' and installs NOTHING -- TJ-GAP-087). A quickstart nobody has re-run on a fresh machine is a hypothesis, not onboarding.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) ONE entry point (a single page, linked from README top) that takes a stranger from nothing to a blocked command and a running jail: install, first verdict, first jailed command, how to read the capability probes, uninstall. 2) The dogfood/QA lane executes it VERBATIM on a fresh bunker agent (no dev-host shortcuts, no hidden steps) and the transcript is attached to this row; a failure re-opens the row. 3) It states plainly what the reader gets on their host (the capability matrix from DOC-9) rather than implying universal containment. 4) Measured time budget stated and met (target: under 5 minutes from clone to first blocked command). 5) Every command in the page is copy-pasteable as written: no placeholders, no '<your-user>' gaps, and the absolute-path installer case works (TJ-GAP-087) or the page does not use it. 6) The page is pinned by a doc test so its commands cannot rot silently.", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:29:13-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (Bane: easy onboarding + docs that speak about BOTH projects)", "attempts": 0, "counterpart": "COUNTERPART (bunker): GAP-180 \u2014 new-user onboarding proven on a fresh server (spawn \u2192 connect \u2192 verify which layer contains you). The two pages must agree on the composed story and share the same per-layer numbers; a change to one must be mirrored in the other."}
 {"id": "DOC-9", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "title": "[P2] Docs that speak about BOTH projects: the composed stack (terminal-jail inside a bunker agent) with one shared per-layer guarantee matrix, and explicit per-layer attribution", "description": "Bane 2026-10-01: the projects are building blocks -- sometimes only terminal-jail is wanted, sometimes only bunker, and the power is the composition, with the tool running INSIDE a bunker for more security. Today neither project documents the other: the tool's README describes backends and rules but never mentions running inside a bunker agent, and its per-host limits are prose scattered across README / COMPATIBILITY.md / docs/backend-parity.md. The composed configuration is also measurably broken right now (TJ-GAP-089), which makes honest documentation more important, not less: a reader must be able to tell which layer enforces what. SHARED PER-LAYER MATRIX (measured 2026-10-01, host = Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31, apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1; container = `docker run --rm python:3.11-slim`): LAYER 1 platform/container -- own PID namespace (inode differs), private procfs (/proc entries = 4, /proc/1 = bash), lifecycle owned by the platform, uid 0 inside with the container's capability set. LAYER 2 tool on a plain host -- per-command verdict with provenance (block exit 126 + rule_id), seccomp filter (Seccomp: 2, CapEff 0 with --seccomp), identity scrub (HOME=/nonexistent USER=nobody), private /proc ONLY via the bwrap backend (5 entries, /proc/1 = bwrap); the unshare --user path exposes the HOST view (1084 entries, /proc/1 = systemd), and on this host class the mapped-root route to a private procfs is closed (uid_map EPERM). LAYER 3 composed (tool inside the platform) -- MEASURED BROKEN TODAY: the firewall half works inside the container (rm -rf / -> blocked, rule_id builtin-rm-rf-root, rc=126) but the tool's own jail refuses to run any command ('namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)') because the container grants no CAP_SYS_ADMIN and its seccomp profile blocks the inner user namespace. Tracked as terminal-jail TJ-GAP-089 + bunker GAP-179.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A 'Composed deployment' section in this repo that states the three configurations (tool alone, platform alone, BOTH) and what each is for, with a runnable quickstart for the composed case that names the platform's requirement (inner user namespace permitted) and what happens when it is not. 2) The SHARED per-layer matrix (layer | who enforces | measured evidence | what the other layer does NOT give you) appears in BOTH repos with identical numbers -- one source of truth, cross-linked, and each copy names the other file. 3) Per-layer attribution is explicit: the tool must never be documented as providing a property the platform actually provides (the composed case is exactly where that mistake would happen). 4) Numbers are pinned by a check (doc test or probe) so they cannot drift apart between the repos. 5) Cross-links: bunker's counterpart row, TJ-GAP-089 (composed mode), TJ-GAP-088 (private /proc ownership), TJ-GAP-082 (Landlock tier), TJ-GAP-090 (onboarding).", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:29:13-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (Bane: easy onboarding + docs that speak about BOTH projects)", "attempts": 0, "counterpart": "COUNTERPART (bunker): DOC-043 \u2014 the composed-stack doc on the platform side, carrying the SAME per-layer guarantee matrix (identical numbers, cross-linked both ways). Related here: TJ-GAP-089 (composed mode), TJ-GAP-088 (private /proc ownership), TJ-GAP-082 (Landlock tier), TJ-GAP-090 (onboarding).", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "813d5c6", "completed_at": "2026-10-03T20:10:00-05:00", "guard_result": "tier1 tests/lint/secrets PASS on staged diff at commit (guard-20260903T195017 log); judge tier2 PASS b52c78bf; tier1 full FAIL = sibling TJ-GAP-088 WIP (tests/test_composed_mode.py), attributed not mine", "worker_summary": "docs/composed-deployment.md (242 lines): 3 configurations, docker-verified composed quickstart, shared per-layer guarantee matrix (4/5/1084 /proc, /proc/1 identities, CapEff, exit 126 rule_id), attribution warnings, cross-links GAP-179/DOC-043/TJ-GAP-089/088/082/090; README link; pin test tests/test_composed_doc.py 7 passed. Commit 813d5c6 pushed, CI success."}
